WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.803
Security work starts with mapping the MCP environment.

2
00:00:03.803 --> 00:00:18.448
The diagram should show the AI host application, MCP client, MCP servers, tools, resources, prompt templates, user identity, service identity, downstream APIs, local files, cloud services, secrets, and tokens.

3
00:00:18.448 --> 00:00:28.076
It should distinguish local servers from remote servers because local execution and remote service access create different risk profiles.

4
00:00:28.076 --> 00:00:39.242
A local server may touch files or processes on the user's machine, while a remote server may rely on network trust, tenant isolation, and external service controls.

5
00:00:39.242 --> 00:00:44.744
A useful architecture diagram shows data flow, authority flow, and action flow.

6
00:00:44.744 --> 00:00:53.078
Data flow answers what information enters the model context, tool arguments, logs, resources, and downstream services.

7
00:00:53.078 --> 00:00:57.932
Authority flow answers which identity or token is used when a tool acts.

8
00:00:57.932 --> 00:01:04.324
Action flow answers what can be read, written, sent, created, deleted, approved, or changed.

9
00:01:04.324 --> 00:01:06.590
These flows do not always line up.

10
00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:16.946
A user may ask a question, the model may propose an action, and a tool may execute under a service account with broader permissions than the user expected.

11
00:01:16.946 --> 00:01:26.170
Trust boundaries appear between user input, model output, tool metadata, tool execution, external content, and downstream systems.

12
00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:28.759
User input is not automatically safe.

13
00:01:28.759 --> 00:01:31.996
Model output is not automatically authorized.

14
00:01:31.996 --> 00:01:37.902
Tool descriptions are not neutral; they influence how the model thinks about tool use.

15
00:01:37.902 --> 00:01:43.242
External resources may contain stale, misleading, or hostile instructions.

16
00:01:43.242 --> 00:01:52.304
Downstream systems may enforce their own policies, but the MCP application still decides what to request and what context to expose.

17
00:01:52.304 --> 00:01:56.431
Architecture visibility prevents vague risk discussions.

18
00:01:56.431 --> 00:02:10.590
Instead of saying an agent can use tools, the team can say which host connects to which servers, which tools are available, which resources are exposed, which credentials are used, and where approvals happen.

19
00:02:10.590 --> 00:02:16.740
That clarity supports least privilege, monitoring, incident response, and governance.

20
00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:27.824
Without it, teams end up trusting the model, the tool, the server, and the user interface as one blended boundary, which is exactly what MCP security needs to avoid.
